      "id": 1,
      "text": "Manchester was named by an English settler after Manchester, England, in the early 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manchester%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The original Manchester Methodist church, built in 1856, is listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://aboutstlouis.com/local/communities/manchester-missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The city’s historic Lyceum, restored in 1977, now serves as Manchester’s City Hall and appears on the National Register.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        20,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://aboutstlouis.com/local/communities/manchester-missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Wagonmaker’s House, constructed in the 1840s, is part of a historic district that includes fifteen 19th‑century buildings along Manchester Road.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://aboutstlouis.com/local/communities/manchester-missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Manchester’s annual 2½‑day Celebration features live entertainment, carnival rides, and historical demonstrations like rug making at the Baxter Log Cabin.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://aboutstlouis.com/local/communities/manchester-missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The city grew from a village incorporated in 1950 to a fourth‑class city by 1959, reflecting rapid suburban development west of St. Louis.",
